6) Tennessee Valley Authority, Director of Environmental Research and Development  felt that environmental statement covered the considerations which are pertinent to the proposed policies and guidelines concerning ORV;
7) Federal Communications Commission, Chairman  indicated general agreement with the objectives of the environmental statement. Expressed concern that provisions be made so that common carriers and others who have wire, cable, or radio communications facilities on public lands have reasonable access to such facilities to the extent necessary for construction, operation, and maintenance for such facilities.
8) Department of the Interior;
(a) Bureau of Land Management  Director  felt that environmental statement should contain additional discussion of impacts of various types of ORV and their impacts on different types of terrain; also felt that statement should clarify status of all Interior lands prior to designation of ORV use;
(b) Bureau of Reclamation, Acting Commissioner  indicated general concurrence relative to the adequacy of the subject environmental statement;
(c) Bureau of Sport Fisheries and Wildlife, Acting Deputy Director - felt that environmental statement represented a comprehensive statement setting forth criteria for protection of natural resources on Federal lands. Suggested that statement include definitions of ORV, public lands, etc., and define status of ORV use on public lands which are open to mineral entry and established mining claims. Felt that much of the description of economic impacts in the statement is inappropriate and unnecessary. Pointed out that the "open or closed" policies of each of the bureaus should be consistent. Stated belief that completed studies on noise pollution contain enough information for definite acceptable noise levels to be spelled out, in decibels at measured distances, for different types ORV and outdoor public use areas;
